[12.91, "o", "Distributed Consensus Protocol Overview\r\n---------------------------------------\r\n\r\nModern distributed systems rely on state machine replication protocols such as Raft [1] and Paxos [2] to guarantee linearizable consistency across unreliable network partitions.\r\n\r\n### Key Protocol Attributes\r\n\r\n+-------------+---------------------+------------------+----------------+\r\n| Algorithm   | Leader Model        | Fault Tolerance  | Complexity     |\r\n+-------------+---------------------+------------------+----------------+\r\n| Raft        | Strong Leader       | (N-1)/2 failures | Understandable |\r\n| Multi-Paxos | Weak / Multi-Leader | (N-1)/2 failures | High           |\r\n| EPaxos      | Leaderless          | (N-1)/2 failures | Very High      |\r\n+-------------+---------------------+------------------+----------------+\r\n\r\nFor more architectural details, refer to the etcd documentation [3] and the MIT 6.824 Distributed Systems course [4].\r\n\r\n[1] https://raft.github.io/\r\n[2] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paxos_(computer_science)\r\n[3] https://etcd.io/docs/\r\n[4] https://pdos.csail.mit.edu/6.824/\r\n"]
